  "body": "**Stacked on #640** (#630, control-point posting and the built-in Lathe's X floor of -2.5). Review only this PR's own commit, `dd45a6c4`.\n\n**P1 (#647):** the machine travel check compared each move's **world X** with a lathe's X band. Turning kernels carry their radius on the **Y meridian**, so a lathe radial over-travel was never judged. It also judged the nose-centre cache, not the control point the post writes.\n\n## What changed\n- **New `crates/app/src/machine/lathe_stroke.rs`.** On lathe-kind machines only, each op is judged on **`posting::nose::posted_path`**, the exact stream the post gets: the control point for the nose-centre turning ops, and the cache for the others.\n  - **X** is the signed offset from the axis on the path's meridian, read as `fanuc-lathe.cps` `radiusOf` reads it, with the same 0.0005 tolerance. Past centre is negative and meets the floor.\n  - **Z** is relative to the origin.\n  - **Arcs** about the spindle axis are judged on their radius, with arc quarter points included.\n  - **Any point off every meridian** is judged three ways: on signed world X, on its radius, and on Y against any Y band. No single reading can clear it.\n  - **Refusal wording** is unchanged: \"<op>: move i reaches v in X, the stroke is lo..hi\".\n  - **When the post refuses the orientation**, the check falls back to the nose-centre cache, so the op is still judged.\n- **`machine/mod.rs` (+9 lines):** calls the lathe judge first and skips the world-X block for lathe machines. Mill and mill-turn checks are unchanged.\n- **Decision record:** `docs/decisions/647-lathe-stroke-radius.md`.\n\n## How tested (`lathe_stroke`, now 10 tests)\n- **New:**\n  - `a_facing_control_point_below_the_x_floor_is_refused_by_name`: with the floor at -0.5, Face1 on the Y meridian is refused with \"reaches -0.800 in X\". It fails without the fix.\n  - `a_turning_pass_beyond_the_x_ceiling_is_refused_by_name`: X max 9 on an r10 bar refuses Rough1. It fails without the fix.\n  - `a_facing_control_point_on_the_x_floor_passes`: a guard at the band edge.\n  - Unit test: `machine::lathe_stroke::tests::the_meridian_radius_is_read_signed`.\n- **Updated:** `a_clearance_past_the_lathe_carriage_still_refuses_by_name` now expects Rough1 at Z 634.200, the control point (r 0.8 below the nose centre), not 635.000. Mill-turn still expects 635.000.\n- **Gate:** conformance, golden_cl, every lathe_* and turning_* suite (including thread), machine, machine_edit, machine_library, posting and tormach_machines pass. Clippy is clean.\n\n## Interactions\n- **#643 (#631):** `mod.rs` merges cleanly. After both merge, lathe machines are judged here first, so #643's `spindle_arc` branch goes unused on lathes; a follow-up can remove it.\n- **#643 vs #640:** separately from this PR, #643's `lathe_thread_travel` fixture states `\"min\": 0.0`, and #640 moves the built-in floor to -2.5. Whichever merges second updates that string.\n- **#644:** its converted groove and part paths are judged automatically, through the same `posted_path`.\n\nChangelog: On a lathe, the machine travel check now judges the posted radius — the control point, on the path's own meridian, signed past centre — so a turning or facing over-travel in X is refused by name instead of passing unseen (#647)\n\nFixes #647\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAgjpiZbXSvmXLvdmL\n",
